<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eadgyth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 910–946</span> · Also known as Edith of England</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eadgyth, also known as Edith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> of the English and the first wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>. Married around 930, she was respected for her piety and generosity, and she died in 946. She was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, her dower city, where her remains were identified in the cathedral in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eadgyth.jpg" alt="Eadgyth" width="250" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Acoma · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edita_250.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
